var SaltCore=SaltCore||{};SaltCore["journey-deity-rhiannon"]=(()=>{var t=Object.defineProperty;var r=Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor;var h=Object.getOwnPropertyNames;var d=Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty;var l=(n,e)=>{for(var o in e)t(n,o,{get:e[o],enumerable:!0})},y=(n,e,o,i)=>{if(e&&typeof e=="object"||typeof e=="function")for(let a of h(e))!d.call(n,a)&&a!==o&&t(n,a,{get:()=>e[a],enumerable:!(i=r(e,a))||i.enumerable});return n};var u=n=>y(t({},"__esModule",{value:!0}),n);var f={};l(f,{journey:()=>s});var s={id:"deity-rhiannon",kind:"deity",paths:["celtic","wheel"],deityRef:"traditional/deity/rhiannon",title:"Rhiannon: The Lady on the White Horse",summary:"A month with Rhiannon of the Mabinogi: the lady no horse could catch, the bag that could never be filled, her son stolen on May Eve and the years she spent at the horse-block, her birds that sang at Harlech, and the scholarly debates about horse goddesses and sovereignty, with a self-dedication made in birdsong at the turning of the light.",welcome:`This journey takes about a lunar month. You will read five lessons, set up a shrine, keep nine dawns or dusks of listening and offering, and decide whether to dedicate yourself to Rhiannon.

It is a self-dedication, not an initiation, and it does not make you a member of any group or order. Rhiannon is one of the most vivid women in medieval literature, but she is never called a goddess in the Welsh tales, and the theory that she was once a great horse and sovereignty goddess, though widely held, is a scholarly reconstruction. This journey keeps the tale and the theory apart. Her story also includes the loss of a newborn and a false accusation; go gently, and pause whenever you need to.`,about:"Rhiannon is the heroine of the First and Third Branches of the Mabinogi, the medieval Welsh tales of Pwyll and of Manawydan, written down in the eleventh or twelfth century. She first appears riding a great white horse past the mound of Gorsedd Arberth in Dyfed, south-west Wales, at a slow and even pace that no rider can catch, until Pwyll, prince of Dyfed, asks her to stop. She has come to choose him. She outwits the rival suitor Gwawl with a magic bag, marries Pwyll, and gives rich gifts to all the nobles of the land. When her newborn son vanishes on May Eve, her women smear her with a puppy\u2019s blood and accuse her of eating him, and she accepts a penance: to sit by the horse-block at the gate for years, telling her story to strangers and offering to carry them on her back like a horse. The boy, raised by Teyrnon in Gwent, comes home as Pryderi, and she is freed. Later, as wife of Manawydan, she follows her son into an enchanted fortress without fear, and is made to wear the collars of asses until Manawydan breaks the spell. Her birds sing for the companions at Harlech for seven years. Scholars have long argued that her name comes from a Celtic *R\u012Bganton\u0101, \u201Cgreat queen\u201D, and that she preserves a horse goddess related to Epona and to the Irish Macha; others are more cautious. Celtic polytheists and Pagans honour her today as a lady of sovereignty, horses, birdsong, patience under injustice, and the otherworld.",notes:["Rhiannon is a character in medieval tales, not a goddess named in any medieval source. Many scholars think she was once a goddess; that is a well-supported theory, not a fact.","Her identification with Epona, or with the Irish Macha, is a scholarly comparison. The parallels are real and worth knowing; they do not prove the three are one.","Her story includes the disappearance of a newborn baby, a false accusation of killing him, and years of public penance. If this touches your own experience of infant loss, postnatal illness or false accusation, go gently, and seek real support alongside devotion.","Welsh is a living language and the Mabinogi is living Welsh heritage. Learn the names (Rhiannon is said roughly \u201Chree-ANN-on\u201D, with a breathy rh), read a good translation, and listen to Welsh voices.","Offerings for wild birds must be safe for them: plain seed, no salted or bready scraps in quantity, and clean feeders. Never feed horses that are not yours without the owner\u2019s permission."],holyDays:["bealtaine","beltane","samhain_gaelic","samhain"],stages:[{kind:"threshold",title:"On the mound",intro:"Begin as Pwyll did, by sitting on the mound and waiting for a wonder. Make a place for Rhiannon, then greet her at dawn or dusk, when the birds sing, and ask her, courteously, to stay for you.",days:2,lessons:[],practices:[{id:"rhiannon-shrine",title:"Set up a shrine near birdsong",instructions:"Choose a shelf or windowsill where you can hear birds, or near a window that faces the morning or evening light. Lay down a white, gold or green cloth. Put a white or gold candle in a safe holder on a heatproof dish. Add a white horse image or figure, and a bird image, a feather you found, or a small bell. Put an apple on a small plate, and a little dish for oats or honey. A stone such as moonstone or pearl suits her, though the link is modern. Many devotees also add a small bag or pouch, for the bag that could never be filled. If you can, hang or place a bird feeder where you can see it from the shrine.",times:1,prompt:"What did you put on her shrine, and what can you hear from it?"}],rite:{title:"Introduction to Rhiannon",summary:"At dawn or dusk, light her candle, listen to the birds, offer an apple and honey, and ask her, as Pwyll did, to stay for you.",minutes:20,ingredients:[{name:"A white or gold candle in a safe holder",ref:"traditional/candle/white"},{name:"An apple"},{name:"A spoonful of honey on a small dish"},{name:"A white horse or bird image"},{name:"A small bell",ref:"traditional/tool/bell",optional:!0},{name:"A sprig of rose or chamomile",ref:"traditional/herb/rose",optional:!0}],preparation:"Choose a time near dawn or dusk. Open a window if you can, so that birdsong comes in. Wash your hands and set out the apple and honey. Sit quietly for a few minutes first, the way Pwyll sat on the mound of Gorsedd Arberth waiting for a wonder.",steps:[{title:"Sit on the mound",instructions:"Sit before the shrine. Listen to the birds for a minute without doing anything else. In the tale, whoever sat on the mound would either be wounded or see a wonder; Pwyll chose to risk it.",minutes:2},{title:"Light the candle",instructions:"Light the candle. Ring the bell once, if you have one.",spoken:"Rhiannon, lady on the white horse, I light this flame for you.",light:!0},{title:"Ask her to stay",instructions:"In the tale, no one could catch Rhiannon by chasing her. Pwyll had only to ask. Ask her courteously.",spoken:"Lady, for the sake of the one you love best, stay for me."},{title:"Offer apple and honey",instructions:"Set the apple and the honey before her image.",spoken:"Here is fruit and sweetness, freely given."},{title:"Say who you are",instructions:"Tell her your name and why you have come. Rhiannon chose her own husband and spoke her mind; speak yours plainly."},{title:"Listen",instructions:"Sit and listen to the birds, or to the silence. Notice what you feel.",minutes:5},{title:"Close",instructions:"Thank her and snuff the candle.",spoken:"Rhiannon, thank you for staying. I will come to the mound again.",snuff:!0}],closing:"Leave the offerings overnight or for the day. Then cut the apple and put it out for birds, or compost it, and dissolve the honey in water and pour it onto the earth. Put out a little plain birdseed for wild birds, in her name.",safety:"Keep the candle on a heatproof dish away from curtains, especially at an open window, and never leave it unattended. Honey must not be given to babies under one year old; keep it away from small children."}},{kind:"study",title:"Learning who she is",intro:"Four lessons on Rhiannon: her coming to Pwyll and the bag of Gwawl; the loss of her son and her penance; her life with Manawydan and her birds; and the scholarly debates about horse goddesses and sovereignty. Read one every day or two.",days:8,lessons:[{id:"rhiannon-pwyll",title:"The lady no one could catch: Rhiannon and Pwyll",summary:"Rhiannon\u2019s arrival at Gorsedd Arberth, how she chose her husband, the trick of the bag, and what kind of woman the tale shows.",minutes:8,sections:[{heading:"The wonder on the mound",body:`The First Branch of the Mabinogi is the story of Pwyll, prince of Dyfed. One day, after a feast at his court at Arberth (Narberth in Pembrokeshire), he climbs a mound called Gorsedd Arberth. Anyone who sits there, his men warn him, will either receive wounds and blows or see a wonder.

He sees a wonder. In Lady Charlotte Guest\u2019s translation, it is \u201Ca lady, on a pure white horse of large size, with a garment of shining gold around her, coming along the highway\u201D, and the horse seems \u201Cto move at a slow and even pace\u201D. Pwyll sends a man after her. The faster the man rides, the further away she is, though she never seems to go faster. The next day, the same. On the third day Pwyll rides after her himself, at full gallop, and still cannot gain on her.`},{heading:"\u201CStay for me\u201D",body:`At last Pwyll does what no one had thought of. He calls out: \u201CO maiden, for the sake of him whom thou best lovest, stay for me.\u201D And she answers, with the dry wit that runs through all her speeches: \u201CI will stay gladly,\u201D said she, \u201Cand it were better for thy horse hadst thou asked it long since.\u201D

She tells him who she is: \u201CI am Rhiannon, the daughter of Heveydd H\xEAn.\u201D She is being married against her will to another man, and she has come to choose Pwyll instead. She sets the date: a year from that night, at her father\u2019s court. It is one of the great scenes in Welsh literature, and it shows everything at once: Rhiannon cannot be captured by force, only asked; and she is the one who chooses.`},{heading:"The bag that could not be filled",body:`At the wedding feast, a stranger asks Pwyll for a boon, and Pwyll rashly promises anything. The stranger is Gwawl son of Clud, the suitor Rhiannon had refused, and he asks for her. Rhiannon is blunt: \u201CNever did man make worse use of his wits than thou hast done.\u201D Then she saves the situation. She gives Pwyll a small magic bag and a plan.

A year later, at the second wedding feast, Pwyll arrives dressed as a beggar and asks Gwawl only to fill his little bag with food. Nothing fills it. It will be full, he says, only when a great lord treads the food down with both feet and says, \u201CEnough has been put therein.\u201D Gwawl steps in; Pwyll pulls the bag over his head and ties it, and his men beat the bag. This, the tale says, was the first game of Badger in the Bag. Gwawl gives up his claim, and Rhiannon marries Pwyll.`},{heading:"A generous queen",body:`Back in Dyfed, Rhiannon gives gifts to all the nobles who come to greet her, men and women. In Guest\u2019s words, there was \u201Cnone to whom Rhiannon did not give some rich gift, either a bracelet, or a ring, or a precious stone. And they ruled the land prosperously both that year and the next.\u201D

In early Welsh and Irish society, gift-giving was how a lord or lady bound people to them and showed their worth. A queen who gives generously makes the land prosper. Many scholars see this as a sign of her older role as a sovereignty figure. Even read simply as a story, it shows a woman of authority, wit and largeness of heart.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"The First Branch, Pwyll Pendefig Dyfed, tells of Gorsedd Arberth, the white horse, \u201Cstay for me\u201D, Rhiannon\u2019s choice of Pwyll, the bag of Gwawl and her gifts. It survives in the White Book of Rhydderch and the Red Book of Hergest (fourteenth century). Guest\u2019s translation dates from the 1840s.",unknown:"We do not know what older stories lie behind the tale or whether Rhiannon was once worshipped. Her father Hyfaidd Hen and her otherworldly origin are only lightly sketched. Reading her gift-giving as a sovereignty sign is interpretation."},inPractice:"Devotees often take \u201Cstay for me\u201D as the model of approach to her: courteous asking rather than pursuit. Many honour her as a patron of women who choose their own lives, and of wit and good counsel.",reflect:["What in your life have you been chasing at full gallop that might stay if you simply asked?","Where have you spoken rashly, as Pwyll did, and who helped you put it right?"],further:["Lady Charlotte Guest, trans., The Mabinogion, \u201CPwyll Prince of Dyved\u201D (public domain, Project Gutenberg).","Sioned Davies, trans., The Mabinogion (Oxford World\u2019s Classics, 2007), the First Branch."],library:["traditional/deity/rhiannon","traditional/deity/epona"]},{id:"rhiannon-horseblock",title:"The horse-block: the lost child and the long penance",summary:"Pryderi\u2019s disappearance on May Eve, the false accusation, Rhiannon\u2019s penance at the gate, and Teyrnon\u2019s mare.",minutes:8,sections:[{heading:"The night the child vanished",body:`For three years Rhiannon has no child, and the nobles press Pwyll to put her aside. Then she bears a son. On the night of his birth, six women are set to watch over mother and baby. They all fall asleep, and when they wake, the baby is gone.

Terrified of being punished, the women make a plan. They kill some puppies from a staghound\u2019s litter, smear the blood on Rhiannon\u2019s face and hands while she sleeps, and lay the bones beside her. When she wakes and asks for her son, they tell her she has eaten him, and that they have the bruises from trying to stop her. Rhiannon pleads with them to tell the truth, promising to protect them: \u201CFor pity\u2019s sake,\u201D said Rhiannon, \u201Cyou will receive no evil by telling the truth.\u201D They will not.`},{heading:"The penance",body:`Pwyll refuses to divorce her, but she must do penance. Rather than argue with the six women, she accepts it. In Guest\u2019s words, she was to remain at Narberth \u201Cuntil the end of seven years, and that she should sit every day near unto a horseblock that was without the gate. And that she should relate the story to all who should come there, whom she might suppose not to know it already; and that she should offer the guests and strangers, if they would permit her, to carry them upon her back into the palace. But it rarely happened that any would permit.\u201D

It is a punishment designed to humiliate: to tell your own false shame to every stranger, and to offer to carry them like a beast of burden. And it is the image at the heart of her devotion today. She sits at the gate year after year, enduring injustice with dignity, and almost no one will let her carry them.`},{heading:"Teyrnon\u2019s mare",body:`Meanwhile, in Gwent, the lord Teyrnon has a beautiful mare who foals every May Eve, and every year the foal vanishes. \u201CThis is the night of the first of May,\u201D he tells his wife, and he sits up armed in the stable. The mare foals. A great claw reaches in at the window and seizes the colt. Teyrnon cuts off the arm, runs out after the monster in the dark, and comes back to find a baby boy wrapped in silk lying at the door.

He and his wife raise the child, who grows at a great rate and loves horses. The colt born that night is given to him. When Teyrnon hears the story of Rhiannon, he sees the boy\u2019s likeness to Pwyll, and brings him home to Narberth.`},{heading:"The end of her trouble",body:`They find Rhiannon sitting by the horse-block, and she offers to carry them in; they refuse. Teyrnon tells the story and presents her son. \u201CI declare to Heaven,\u201D said Rhiannon, \u201Cthat if this be true, there is indeed an end to my trouble.\u201D The druid Pendaran Dyfed seizes on her word for trouble or anxiety, pryder, and the boy is named Pryderi.

Teyrnon is rewarded and the false accusation is undone. The tale says nothing of what happened to the six women. What it does say is that Rhiannon\u2019s endurance did not go unanswered: the truth came home, carried on a horse.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"The First Branch tells of the vanishing of the child, the false accusation, the seven-year penance at the horse-block, Teyrnon\u2019s mare foaling on May Eve, the return of the boy and his naming as Pryderi. The quotations are from Guest\u2019s translation.",unknown:"We do not know who or what took the child, which the tale never explains, or why the penance takes the form it does. The horse imagery (carrying guests, the mare and foal) is the strongest basis for the horse-goddess theory, but the tale does not say she is a horse goddess."},inPractice:"Many devotees turn to Rhiannon in times of false accusation, slander or unjust treatment, and some in grief for a lost child. Acts of justice for the wrongly accused, such as supporting appeals, legal aid or prisoner charities, are common offerings.",reflect:["Have you ever carried a shame that was not yours? What helped, or would have helped?","Who in your world sits at the horse-block today, telling a story no one believes? What could you do for them?"],further:["Sioned Davies, The Four Branches of the Mabinogi (1993).","Catherine McKenna, \u201CThe Theme of Sovereignty in Pwyll\u201D, Bulletin of the Board of Celtic Studies 29 (1980), 35\u201352; reprinted in C. W. Sullivan III, ed., The Mabinogi: A Book of Essays (1996)."],library:["traditional/deity/rhiannon","traditional/herb/chamomile"]},{id:"rhiannon-manawydan",title:"Fearless at the gate: Rhiannon, Manawydan and her birds",summary:"Rhiannon\u2019s second marriage, the enchantment of Dyfed, the golden bowl, the ass-collars, and the birds that sang at Harlech.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"The birds of Rhiannon",body:`In the Second Branch, the tale of Branwen, the giant king Bendigeidfran is dying after a terrible war in Ireland. He orders his companions to cut off his head and carry it home, and tells them what will happen on the way: \u201CIn Harlech you will be feasting seven years, the birds of Rhiannon singing unto you the while.\u201D

Another Welsh tale, Culhwch and Olwen, lists the birds of Rhiannon among the impossible things to be won, and says their song can wake the dead and lull the living to sleep. They are otherworld birds, whose song stops time and heals grief for a while. This is why birdsong is offered to her, and why many devotees ask her for rest, sleep and comfort in mourning.`},{heading:"Rhiannon and Manawydan",body:`In the Third Branch, after Pwyll\u2019s death, Pryderi gives his mother in marriage to his friend Manawydan son of Ll\u0177r, one of the companions who had heard her birds at Harlech. Unusually for a medieval tale, the marriage is presented as one of mutual liking: they talk together, and are pleased with each other.

Then Dyfed is enchanted. After a peal of thunder and a mist, every person, house and animal in the land vanishes, except Rhiannon, Manawydan, Pryderi and his wife Cigfa. The four live by hunting, then wander England working as craftsmen, saddlers, shield-makers and shoemakers, and are driven out every time by jealous locals. It is a story about surviving loss and making a living with your hands.`},{heading:"She was nothing daunted",body:`One day Pryderi and Manawydan follow a shining white boar into a fortress that has appeared out of nowhere. Manawydan, wary, stays outside. Pryderi goes in, touches a golden bowl hanging on chains, and is stuck fast and struck dumb. Manawydan goes home and tells Rhiannon.

She does not wait. \u201CAn evil companion hast thou been,\u201D said Rhiannon, \u201Cand a good companion hast thou lost.\u201D And in Guest\u2019s words: \u201CThe gate of the castle she found open. She was nothing daunted, and she went in.\u201D She takes hold of the bowl beside her son, and she too is held fast, and the fortress vanishes with them both. It is the bravest moment in the tale: a mother walking into an enchantment she knows is a trap, to be with her child.`},{heading:"The collars of asses",body:`Manawydan, left with Cigfa, turns farmer, and his wheat is stolen by an army of mice. He catches one, a pregnant female, and prepares to hang it. A series of strangers try to buy its life. The last is Llwyd son of Cil Coed, the enchanter who had cast the spell in revenge for Gwawl and the bag, and the mouse is his wife. Manawydan demands the release of Rhiannon and Pryderi and the lifting of the spell from Dyfed for ever.

Then he asks what their bondage had been. \u201CPryderi has had the knockers of the gate of my palace about his neck, and Rhiannon has had the collars of the asses, after they have been carrying hay, about her neck.\u201D Once again, Rhiannon has been made to bear the burden of a beast. Once again, she comes home.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"The Second Branch mentions the birds of Rhiannon at Harlech; Culhwch and Olwen lists them among the tasks. The Third Branch, Manawydan fab Ll\u0177r, tells of her marriage to Manawydan, the enchantment, the fortress and golden bowl, the mice, and the ass-collars. The quotations are from Guest\u2019s translation.",unknown:"We do not know why the birds are hers, where her otherworld home was, or how far the Third Branch reflects older myth. Manawydan is the Welsh counterpart of the Irish Manann\xE1n mac Lir, but how the two traditions relate is debated."},inPractice:"Devotees offer song and birdsong, feed wild birds, and ask Rhiannon for sleep, rest and comfort in grief. Parents often pray to her for children in danger. Some honour Manawydan with her, as her patient and clever second husband.",reflect:["Where in your life do you need to walk through an open gate, nothing daunted?","What song or sound gives you rest when you are grieving?"],further:["Lady Charlotte Guest, trans., The Mabinogion, \u201CBranwen\u201D and \u201CManawyddan\u201D (public domain).","Sioned Davies, trans., The Mabinogion (2007), including Culhwch and Olwen."],library:["traditional/deity/rhiannon","traditional/deity/manannan","traditional/herb/lavender"],premade:["hedge-dream-pillow","hedge-letting-go-of-the-dead"]},{id:"rhiannon-theory",title:"Great queen and horse goddess? The debates, and how she is honoured today",summary:"The *R\u012Bganton\u0101 theory, comparisons with Epona and Macha, the sovereignty reading, modern communities, and what to be careful of.",minutes:8,sections:[{heading:"The great queen",body:`Since the nineteenth century, scholars have argued that Rhiannon\u2019s name comes from a Celtic *R\u012Bganton\u0101, \u201Cgreat queen\u201D or \u201Cdivine queen\u201D, built from the word for king or queen (Gaulish r\u012Bx, Welsh rhi) with a divine ending like the -ona in Epona. The asterisk means the form is reconstructed: no inscription to a goddess Rigantona has been found. The linguistics are widely accepted as plausible, though not every scholar is convinced.

The Welsh scholar W. J. Gruffydd, in his book Rhiannon (1953), went much further, reconstructing a whole lost myth in which she was a goddess whose son was stolen and returned. Later scholars have been more cautious, but the idea that Rhiannon is a goddess \u201Cin disguise\u201D in the Mabinogi remains mainstream.`},{heading:"Horses, Epona and Macha",body:`The horse imagery around Rhiannon is striking. She rides a horse no one can catch. Her son vanishes the night a mare foals and comes home with a colt. Her penance is to carry people like a horse, and later she wears ass-collars. Many scholars therefore link her with the Gaulish horse goddess Epona, whose name has the same divine ending, and with the Irish Macha, who in one tale is forced to race the king\u2019s horses while pregnant, wins, gives birth to twins and curses the men of Ulster.

These parallels are real and interesting. But they are comparisons, not proof. There is no ancient source saying Rhiannon is Epona, and she and Macha come from different countries and centuries. Some scholars think the horse motifs are older story patterns rather than signs of one goddess. When you honour Rhiannon as a horse goddess, it is honest to say that this is a scholarly theory you find meaningful.`},{heading:"The sovereignty reading",body:`A related theory sees Rhiannon as a sovereignty figure: an otherworld woman who chooses a mortal king, and through marriage to her he gains a prosperous rule. In Irish tradition, sovereignty is often a woman who offers a drink or a union to the rightful king. Rhiannon chooses Pwyll, gives gifts that make the land prosper, and when she and her son are taken away the land of Dyfed itself is laid waste, and returns only when she does.

The Celticist Catherine McKenna, in a 1980 article, and others have explored this reading carefully. It fits the tale well, and many devotees honour her as a lady of sovereignty: rule over oneself as well as over land. Again, it is an interpretation, though a strong one.`},{heading:"Honouring her today, and what to avoid",body:`Rhiannon is honoured by Welsh-focused Celtic polytheists, who start from the Mabinogi and the landscape of Dyfed; by Druids; by Wiccans and Goddess-centred Pagans, who often see her as a horse and moon goddess; and by many people drawn to her story of endurance. Reconstructionists usually keep to what the tales say and flag the theories; eclectic Pagans often blend her with Epona. Know which you are doing, and say so.

Avoid presenting Rigantona, the horse goddess or the moon as medieval fact. Avoid turning her penance into a lesson that suffering in silence is holy: in the tale she endures because she has no choice, and the story celebrates the truth coming out. And do not use devotion to her in place of real help. If you or someone you know is being falsely accused, abused or blamed for a loss, find people who can help, and let her stand beside you.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"Scholars have derived her name from a reconstructed *R\u012Bganton\u0101, \u201Cgreat queen\u201D. The horse motifs in her story, the comparison with Epona and Macha, and the sovereignty reading are discussed in mainstream Celtic scholarship, including W. J. Gruffydd (1953) and Catherine McKenna (1980).",unknown:"No inscription to Rigantona exists. We do not know whether Rhiannon was worshipped, whether she was a horse goddess, or whether she, Epona and Macha were ever connected. The sovereignty reading is interpretation. Modern links to the moon are modern."},inPractice:"A typical devotee keeps a shrine with a white horse, birds and apples, offers song and birdseed at dawn or dusk, reads the First and Third Branches regularly, and works for justice for the falsely accused and comfort for the grieving.",reflect:["Does it matter to you whether Rhiannon was once a goddess? Why or why not?","What would sovereignty over your own life look like right now?"],further:["W. J. Gruffydd, Rhiannon: An Inquiry into the Origins of the First and Third Branches of the Mabinogi (1953).","Proinsias Mac Cana, Celtic Mythology (1970).","Miranda Green, Celtic Goddesses (1995), on horse goddesses."],library:["traditional/deity/rhiannon","traditional/deity/epona","traditional/deity/morrigan"]}],practices:[{id:"rhiannon-study-listen",title:"Listen before reading",instructions:"Before each lesson, light her candle and listen to birdsong for two minutes, from a window, a garden or a recording. Say, \u201CRhiannon, stay for me.\u201D Afterwards, note one thing about her that moved you.",times:4,every:"day",prompt:"What about her story moved you today?"}]},{kind:"practice",title:"Nine dawns of birdsong",intro:"Now you put what you have learned into practice. For nine dawns or dusks you will listen to the birds, make a small offering and sing, however simply. Start with the lesson on her days, offerings and the shape of a rite.",days:10,lessons:[{id:"rhiannon-offerings",title:"Song, apples and seed: days, offerings and the shape of a rite",summary:"Her May Eve and Calan Gaeaf, what to offer and how, feeding birds safely, a simple rite, and keeping her shrine.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"Her days",body:`May Eve, the night before Calan Mai, the Welsh first of May, is the night in her story when the mare foals and the lost child is found. In Welsh tradition it was one of the three spirit nights of the year, when the otherworld was close. Many devotees keep it as her great night, alongside Bealtaine and Beltane on the modern calendar.

Calan Gaeaf, the Welsh first of winter on 1 November, with its spirit night on 31 October, suits her otherworld side and her birds that wake the dead. For regular devotion, dawn and dusk are her daily times, when birds sing most. Choose a day of the week to make a fuller offering, and keep it.`},{heading:"What to offer",body:`Song is her first offering. Sing to her, however simply: a lullaby, a hymn, a folk song, a single held note. If you cannot sing, play birdsong, whistle, or ring a small bell. Apples are fitting for a lady of the otherworld; honey and oats are common modern offerings; mead and white wine are often given. Roses suit a queen.

Two offerings come straight from her story. The first is birdseed put out for wild birds, which honours her birds and helps real ones. The second is acts of justice: supporting someone falsely accused, giving to legal aid or a charity for wrongful convictions, or simply believing someone who tells you the truth about what happened to them. Many devotees think that is the offering she values most.`},{heading:"Feeding birds safely",body:`If you feed birds in her name, do it well. Use plain bird seed, sunflower hearts or suet made for birds. Avoid salty food, bread in quantity (it fills birds up without nourishing them), mouldy food, and anything cooked with onion. Never put out loose peanuts in spring, when they can choke nestlings; use a mesh feeder.

Clean feeders and water dishes every week or two, since dirty feeders spread disease among garden birds. Put feeders where cats cannot ambush the birds. And if you keep a cat yourself, a bell on its collar and keeping it in at dawn and dusk will protect the birds whose song you are offering to her.`},{heading:"The shape of a simple rite",body:`A rite for Rhiannon has five parts. First, sit on the mound: sit quietly and listen. Second, light her candle and ask her to stay. Third, offer: apple, honey or oats on the shrine, and seed for the birds outside. Fourth, sing. Fifth, ask and close: ask for patience, justice, rest or comfort, and thank her.

On May Eve, you can expand this: keep watch for a while after dark, as Teyrnon watched his mare, light a candle for lost children and their families, and greet the dawn on May morning with song and, if you like, a dew washing. Leave offerings overnight, then compost fruit and pour honey-water onto the earth.`},{heading:"Keeping her shrine",body:`Keep her shrine bright and simple. Replace the apple before it spoils. Keep the bell clean and ring it when you greet her. Add feathers you find, and a picture of the birds that visit your feeder. In spring, add blossom; in autumn, windfall apples.

The little bag on her shrine can hold slips of paper with the names of people you are praying for, especially those facing false accusation or grief. When their trouble ends, as Rhiannon\u2019s did, take their name out and give thanks.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"In the First Branch the mare foals on May Eve and the child is found that night. May Eve and the eve of Calan Gaeaf were spirit nights (ysbrydnosau) in Welsh folk tradition. The birds of Rhiannon appear in the Second Branch and Culhwch and Olwen. The bird-feeding advice follows current wildlife guidance.",unknown:"No medieval source gives Rhiannon a holy day or offerings. Song, apples, honey and seed as offerings, and the five-part rite, are modern practice drawn from the tales."},inPractice:"Many devotees greet her at dawn or dusk with song, feed wild birds, keep May Eve as her night, and make acts of justice their regular offering.",reflect:["What song will you offer her? Why that one?","Whose name might you put in her bag this month?"],further:["Trefor M. Owen, Welsh Folk Customs (1959), on Calan Mai and Calan Gaeaf.","RSPB guidance on feeding garden birds."],library:["traditional/deity/rhiannon","traditional/herb/rose","traditional/crystal/moonstone"],premade:["hedge-dusk-rite","folk-bealtaine-dew"]}],practices:[{id:"rhiannon-nine-dawns",title:"Nine dawns or dusks of birdsong",instructions:"For nine days, at dawn or dusk, light her candle and listen to the birds for five minutes. Say: \u201CRhiannon, lady on the white horse, stay for me.\u201D Offer a slice of apple or a little honey or oats on her shrine, and put a little seed out for wild birds. Then sing something, however short. Tell her one thing you are enduring, and ask for patience or justice. Snuff the candle. On one of the nine days, do something for someone who has been treated unfairly: listen to them, speak up for them, or give to a charity that helps the wrongly accused.",times:9,every:"day",prompt:"What did you hear, what did you sing, and what did you ask?",premade:"hedge-dusk-rite"}]},{kind:"discernment",title:"At the horse-block",intro:"Before any dedication, look back over the month. A dedication to Rhiannon is a promise to endure with dignity, to speak for the wrongly accused and to keep her in song. Stopping, waiting, or choosing another goddess or path are all good outcomes; she will not be caught by chasing, and she does not hold it against those who ride on.",days:3,lessons:[],practices:[{id:"rhiannon-discern",title:"Sit by a gate",instructions:"Sit by a gate, a doorway or a path where people come and go, as Rhiannon sat by the horse-block. Stay for a while, watching. Think through the questions below. Write your answers when you get home.",times:1,prompt:"Your honest answers to the discernment questions."}],questions:["Did you keep most of the nine dawns or dusks? What does that tell you about the promise you might make?","Are you drawn to Rhiannon of the tales, or to the horse and sovereignty goddess of the theories? Can you be honest about which?","Does her story of loss and false accusation touch something in your own life that needs care from people as well as devotion?","Can you honour her endurance without making a virtue of suffering in silence?","Would you be willing to stand up for someone wrongly accused, as part of honouring her?","Is another deity or path calling you more strongly? Choosing them, or pausing, is a good and honest outcome."]},{kind:"dedication",title:"Dedication at the turning of the light",intro:"If you choose to go on, this is a self-dedication to Rhiannon, made before her in your own words. It is not an initiation into any group, order or tradition, and it does not replace one where they have their own. May Eve or May morning is the fitting time, but any dawn or dusk you are ready will do.",days:1,lessons:[],practices:[],rite:{title:"Self-dedication to Rhiannon",summary:"At dawn or dusk, sit on the mound, ask her to stay, offer fruit and song, make your promise, and listen to her birds.",minutes:40,ingredients:[{name:"A white or gold candle in a safe holder",ref:"traditional/candle/gold"},{name:"Apples and honey"},{name:"A cup of mead, white wine or apple juice"},{name:"Bird seed to put out afterwards"},{name:"A song you have chosen or written for her"},{name:"A small bag or pouch, for your promise"},{name:"A white rose or other white flowers",ref:"traditional/herb/rose",optional:!0},{name:"Your written promise"}],preparation:"Write your promise over several days: how you will honour her (dawn or dusk greetings, a weekly offering, May Eve each year), what you will do for those wrongly accused or grieving, and how you will keep her in song. Make it a year\u2019s promise. Learn your song. On the day, rise before dawn or prepare for dusk. Wash and dress with care. Open a window if you can.",steps:[{title:"Sit on the mound",instructions:"Sit before the shrine in silence and listen to the birds, or the quiet, for a few minutes.",minutes:3},{title:"Light the candle",instructions:"Light the candle. Lay the flowers before her image.",spoken:"Rhiannon, lady on the white horse, queen of Dyfed, I light this flame for you.",light:!0},{title:"Ask her to stay",instructions:"Ask her courteously, as Pwyll did.",spoken:"Lady, for the sake of the one you love best, stay for me. I come to you of my own free will."},{title:"Offer fruit, honey and drink",instructions:"Set the apples, honey and cup before her.",spoken:"Here are fruit, sweetness and drink, freely given, as you gave gifts to all who came to you."},{title:"Sing",instructions:"Sing your song for her. Do not worry about how it sounds."},{title:"Make your promise",instructions:"Read your promise aloud. Say plainly that this is a self-dedication, made in good faith, and that you will keep it as best you can. Fold it and put it in the bag on her shrine.",spoken:"This is my word, given to you. I will speak for the wrongly accused, comfort the grieving, and keep you in song."},{title:"Listen to her birds",instructions:"Sit in silence. Imagine the hall at Harlech, the companions at rest, and the birds of Rhiannon singing so sweetly that time stops.",minutes:10},{title:"Close",instructions:"Thank her and snuff the candle.",spoken:"Rhiannon, thank you for staying. I go out nothing daunted.",snuff:!0}],closing:"Put the bird seed out for wild birds straight away. Leave the other offerings for the day, then compost the fruit and pour the honey and drink, diluted, onto the earth. Keep the bag with your promise on her shrine. Within a week, do one thing for someone wrongly accused or grieving.",safety:"Keep the candle on a heatproof dish away from flowers and curtains, especially at an open window, and never leave it unattended. Keep honey away from babies under one. Pour alcohol away from pets and children, and keep apple seeds and cores out of reach of dogs."}},{kind:"ongoing",title:"Riding on",intro:"The relationship lives in song, in patience and in justice. Here is a rhythm: daily listening, a weekly offering, May Eve and Calan Gaeaf each year, and a yearly renewal.",days:4,lessons:[],practices:[{id:"rhiannon-weekly",title:"Weekly offering and song",instructions:"Once a week, at dawn or dusk, light her candle, offer an apple or honey, put out seed for the birds, and sing. Tell her what you are enduring and what you are grateful for.",times:52,every:"week",prompt:"What did you sing, and what are you enduring or celebrating?"},{id:"rhiannon-justice",title:"Monthly act of justice",instructions:"Once a month, do something for someone wrongly accused, treated unfairly or grieving: listen, speak up, write a letter, give to a relevant charity. Dedicate it to her.",times:12,every:"month",prompt:"Whom did you stand with this month?"},{id:"rhiannon-may-eve",title:"May Eve",instructions:"On May Eve (30 April, or 31 October in the southern hemisphere if you keep the seasons there), keep watch for a while after dark as Teyrnon did, light a candle for lost children and their families, and greet the dawn on May morning with song.",times:1,every:"holy-day",prompt:"What did you watch for, and what came home this year?",premade:"folk-bealtaine-dew"},{id:"rhiannon-renew",title:"Yearly renewal",instructions:"On May Eve or the anniversary of your dedication, take your promise from her bag and read it. Say honestly what you kept. Make a new promise for the next year, or release the old one with thanks.",times:1,every:"holy-day",prompt:"What did you endure, and what did you set right, this year?"}]}],sources:["Lady Charlotte Guest, trans., The Mabinogion, \u201CPwyll Prince of Dyved\u201D, \u201CBranwen the daughter of Llyr\u201D and \u201CManawyddan the son of Llyr\u201D, Project Gutenberg: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/5160 (quoted: the lady on the white horse; \u201CO maiden, for the sake of him whom thou best lovest, stay for me\u201D; \u201CI will stay gladly\u2026\u201D; \u201CI am Rhiannon, the daughter of Heveydd H\xEAn\u201D; \u201CNever did man make worse use of his wits\u2026\u201D; \u201CEnough has been put therein\u201D; her gifts; \u201CFor pity\u2019s sake\u2026\u201D; the penance at the horseblock; \u201CThis is the night of the first of May\u201D; \u201Cthere is indeed an end to my trouble\u201D; the birds of Rhiannon at Harlech; \u201CAn evil companion hast thou been\u2026\u201D; \u201CShe was nothing daunted, and she went in\u201D; the collars of the asses).","Sioned Davies, trans., The Mabinogion (Oxford World\u2019s Classics, 2007).","W. J. Gruffydd, Rhiannon (University of Wales Press, 1953).","Catherine McKenna, \u201CThe Theme of Sovereignty in Pwyll\u201D, Bulletin of the Board of Celtic Studies 29 (1980), 35\u201352; reprinted in C. W. Sullivan III, ed., The Mabinogi: A Book of Essays (1996).","Trefor M. Owen, Welsh Folk Customs (1959).","Proinsias Mac Cana, Celtic Mythology (1970)."]};return u(f);})();
